62 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 278 0 R
/E (Much of the market story over the last three weeks has been on oil, with Brent rising above $100 and politicians trying to reopen the Strait of Hormuz. However, this framing ignores the more important change. What is happening is not just an energy shock, but a disturbance in the movement of commodities themselves. The effective shutdown of Hormuz has revealed a more fragile reality: global supply systems fail not because manufacturing ceases, but when transportation becomes unreliable, uninsurable, or simply impossible.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
63 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 278 0 R
/E (The Strait transports almost one-fifth of world oil and gas, but it also transports intermediate commodities, chemicals, and inputs that support industrial production. As shipping firms cease routes, declare force majeure, and dump goods at other ports, the system fragments rather than collapses. Cargoes are being redirected to India, East Africa, and Southeast Asia, ports are congested, and prices are cascading down supply chains in ways that are just now becoming understood.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
64 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 278 0 R
/E (Shipping as the Binding Constraint)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
65 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 278 0 R
/E (This crisis is defined by logistical paralysis rather than supply destruction. Container shipping has effectively reached what industry)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
66 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 279 0 R
/E (players refer to as the "wild west" era. Freight charges into the Gulf have increased dramatically, war-risk insurance premiums have risen, and shipping companies have begun dumping cargo at the nearest feasible ports, forcing customers to sail the remaining leg themselves.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
67 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 279 0 R
/E (This is a crucial shift. Shipping is sometimes viewed as a passive conduit for trade, but it really serves as the operating system of globalisation. When that system becomes uncertain, trade does not automatically redirect. Instead, it slows, fragments, and becomes much more costly. Around 90% of global products go by water, and even minor disruptions at chokepoints like Hormuz cause bottlenecks that spread across continents.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
68 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 279 0 R
/E (The end outcome is a cascade of second-order consequences. Alternative centres like as Mumbai and Singapore are experiencing increased congestion, longer wait times, and equipment shortages. Even while routes are nominally available, the lack of insurance coverage and the risk to personnel make them commercially useless. In this setting, the restriction is no longer supply, but rather the desire and capacity to transfer it.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
