/P 282 0 R
/E (Aluminium)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
72 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (This is especially evident in aluminium markets, where the move from risk to actual disruption has been swift. The Middle East contributes for over 10% of global aluminium output, but its smelters rely on steady inputs of alumina and electricity. With shipping routes interrupted and supplies often covering only a few weeks, production cutbacks are no longer an option.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
73 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (Smelters around the Gulf have already halted shipments and declared force majeure, with several operations facing complete shutdowns. This demonstrates the highly interconnected structure of contemporary metals manufacturing. Aluminium is not simply mined and shipped; it is part of a continuous chain of inputs and outputs, all of which depend on prompt delivery. When shipment breaks down, the chain unravels swiftly.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
74 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (Even if transit via Hormuz restarts, aluminium markets may take much longer to normalise. Unlike oil tankers, containerised metal flows rely on network balance and equipment location, both of which have already been affected. This creates a delayed tightening effect, in which supply restraints linger long after the original shock.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
75 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (Sulphur and the Hidden Supply Chain)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
76 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (While aluminium symbolises the obvious edge of disruption, the more relevant narrative is in commodities that markets prefer to overlook. Sulphur, a byproduct of oil and gas processing, has emerged as a major bottleneck with far-reaching consequences.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
77 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 284 0 R
/E (The Gulf accounts for around 45% of global sulphur exports, and with ships virtually suspended, prices have risen while physical commerce has paused. This is important not because sulphur is valuable, but because it is required in the process. It is utilised in fertilisers, chemicals, semiconductor fabrication, and, most importantly, metal leaching, including copper and nickel.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
78 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 284 0 R
/E (This causes a layered supply shock. Disruptions in sulphur shipments are already affecting fertiliser markets, creating worries about food security, but the indirect consequences on metals are just beginning to emerge. Copper production in locations like the Central African Copperbelt is strongly reliant on sulphuric acid, and when supplies tighten, production costs and output hazards rise.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
79 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 284 0 R
/E (In this sense, the shock spreads not linearly, but systemically. A disturbance in a small chemical sector spreads through metals, agriculture, and even technology supply chains, demonstrating how interdependent these systems have become.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
