>>
endobj
90 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (Rising freight and gasoline prices exacerbate these challenges. Tanker rates out of the Gulf have risen, container shipping costs into the area have increased, and fuel surcharges are now accounting for a larger portion of logistical costs. Diesel prices have risen substantially, directly affecting transportation costs on both marine and land-based networks.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (This generates a feedback loop. Higher energy prices enhance transportation expenses, which raises the cost of moving goods, further reducing supply and driving prices up. The longer the interruption lasts, the more these cost pressures integrate into global inflation dynamics.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
92 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (Importantly, the impacts are not uniformly distributed. While certain routes, notably those across the Pacific, remain largely unaffected for the time being, the Indian Ocean and Gulf-linked networks are under extreme stress. These localised disturbances, however, have the potential to spill over into larger trade flows when equipment mismatches and bottlenecks spread across the system.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
