/P 356 0 R
/E (Sowing Scarcity: Fertiliser Supply and the Food Crisis Behind the Iran War)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (When joint US-Israeli attacks on Iran began in late February, global markets descended into chaos. The following closure of the Strait of Hormuz, through which one fifth of all oil and LNG resources are transported, sparked headlines about energy crisis. However, a far more threatening crisis has developed largely unnoticed, fertiliser supply. However, a far more dangerous and largely overlooked crisis has emerged: disruption to fertiliser supply, a threat that directly endangers global food production and security.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (While these direct supply disruptions are dangerous, the indirect consequences are even more damaging. LNG accounts for roughly 70 percent of the production costs of nitrogen-based fertilisers. The recent spike in gas prices as a result of the war has thus made production economically unviable resulting in the closure of hundreds of plants across South Asia, North Africa and Europe.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (This contraction in supply comes just as international demand is predicted to surge with the sowing season in the Norther Hemisphere about to begin.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (Pre-war vulnerabilities have also amplified the current crisis. The 2022 invasion of Ukraine was a huge hit to the sector, eliminating a large amount of output from Russia and Belarus. China has aggressively regulated its fertiliser exports since 2021 with )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 358 0 R
/E (the aim of protecting domestic farmers. As the largest nitrogen fertiliser producer in the world, this is another constriction on the current supply.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 358 0 R
/E (It is approximated that half of global food production is dependant on synthetic fertilisers. This means that all nations will be severely impacted by a shortened supply.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 358 0 R
/E (However as seen in 2022, developing countries and regions which do not have access to their own means of production of fertilisers will be worse effected. These include areas like South America and Sub-Saharan Africa.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
189 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 358 0 R
/E (The Strait of Hormuz closure has created a food security crisis that cannot be ignored. Oil and LNG markets will adjust in the long run, however food production operates on fixed seasonal schedules that cannot be altered. There is no time for alternative suppliers to emerge or production to ramp up. As the war continues and the crisis deepens, it is clear that the fertiliser shortage is not a secondary effect but a primary threat to global food security.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
